<header><p class="st-kicker">imperial city · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Free Imperial City of Wetzlar</h1><p class="st-meta">1180 CE – 1803 CE (approx.) · capital Wetzlar</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Small Hessian imperial city that became home to the Empire's supreme court in 1689, where the young Goethe trained and found the story of Werther.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Wetzlar grew around a collegiate church on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahn">Lahn</a> and received town privileges from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a>, being treated as an imperial city from about 1180. It lay where trade roads crossed the river, and its cloth and leather trade made it prosperous in the 13th century, when work began on the large collegiate church known as the cathedral.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A council under the emperor's protection governed the town, but heavy debts and feuds in the 14th century left it weak, and it relied on neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-nassau">Nassau</a>. Its fortunes revived when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-chamber-court">Imperial Chamber Court</a>, the Reichskammergericht, moved there from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-speyer">Speyer</a> in 1689 and stayed until 1806. Lawyers flocked to the town; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-wolfgang-von-goethe">Goethe</a> worked at the court in 1772, and his stay inspired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-sorrows-of-young-werther">The Sorrows of Young Werther</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1803 Wetzlar lost its freedom and was given to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-theodor-von-dalberg">Karl Theodor von Dalberg</a> as a county. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> assigned it to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia</a> in 1815. It is now a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a> known for optics and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leica-camera">Leica</a> camera.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/free-imperial-city-of-wetzlar.jpg" alt="Free Imperial City of Wetzlar" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Krusto · CC BY 2.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wetzlarskyline.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1180 CE – 1803 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>imperial city</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Wetzlar</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism, then Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Wetzlar</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1492&amp;focus=free-imperial-city-of-wetzlar" aria-label="Free Imperial City of Wetzlar on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4874 422 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Free Imperial City of Wetzlar lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5194 678L5229 694M5194 678L5279 723"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5279 723h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5194 678h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5194 678h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1492&amp;focus=free-imperial-city-of-wetzlar">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
